op": [["`", -0.0016467414097860456], ["*", -6.9883599281311035]]}, {"t": " semantics", "lp": -0.1560099869966507, "top": [[" semantics", -0.1560099869966507], [" values", -2.890362024307251]]}, {"t": " in", "lp": -0.7204232215881348, "top": [[" in", -0.7204232215881348], [" may", -1.3041423559188843]]}, {"t": " the", "lp": -0.009956467896699905, "top": [[" the", -0.009956467896699905], [" recent", -5.576353073120117]]}, {"t": " `", "lp": -0.06628616154193878, "top": [[" `", -0.06628616154193878], [" xe", -4.072126388549805]]}, {"t": "xe", "lp": -0.007871231995522976, "top": [["xe", -0.007871231995522976], ["Xe", -7.380385875701904]]}, {"t": "`", "lp": -0.00030462612630799413, "top": [["`", -0.00030462612630799413], ["2", -10.479001998901367]]}, {"t": " driver", "lp": -0.003819658188149333, "top": [[" driver", -0.003819658188149333], [" (", -6.112712860107422]]}, {"t": " may", "lp": -0.805083155632019, "top": [[" may", -0.805083155632019], [" is", -1.0735195875167847]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are distributed across the GPU's compute engines. This is set via kernel module parameters for the `xe` driver.\n\n## The Modes\n\n**ccs_mode 1** \u2014 *Balanced/Default*\n- Distributes CCS contexts across **all available CCS engines**\n- Provides good load balancing across the compute units\n- This is generally the recommended default for most workloads\n\n**ccs_mode 2** \u2014 *Single CCS / Isolation*\n- Pins contexts to a **single CCS engine** (or restricts usage)\n- Useful for **isolation testing** or when you want deterministic behavior on one engine\n- Can help avoid cross-engine scheduling overhead in specific scenarios\n\n**ccs_mode 4** \u2014 *CCS0 Only / Specific Engine*\n- Forces usage of a **specific CCS engine** (typically CCS0)\n- Used for **debugging** or when you need to target a particular engine explicitly\n\n## When to Use Each\n\n| Mode | Use Case |\n|------|----------|\n| **1** | Default for most workloads \u2014 best general performance |\n| **2** | Testing, isolation, or avoiding certain scheduling issues |\n| **4** | Debugging specific engine behavior, or workloads that benefit from a dedicated engine |\n\n## Practical Notes\n\n- **Most users should leave this at the default (mode 1)** unless debugging\n- These parameters are primarily relevant for **performance tuning** or **troubleshooting** specific issues\n- The exact semantics can vary between driver versions, so check your specific kernel version's documentation\n\n---\n\n**A caveat:** I want to be transparent that my knowledge of the exact `ccs_mode` semantics in the `xe` driver may"}